      "content": "Semiotic clarity \nSemiotic clarity means that every concept is represented by exactly one symbol and every symbol represents \nexactly one concept (Goodman, 1968). \nEvery event class of the event log is represented in a Dotted Chart as a dot with a unique color. Moreover, each \ndot has a unique position. Therefore, the Dotted Chart has maximal semiotic clarity. Nevertheless, it can \nsometimes be appropriate to introduce symbol deficit (i.e., use the same symbol for different concepts) to \nincrease graphic economy (Moody, 2009).",
